Things you will need
---------------------
HTTP Server ( ie IIS or Apache)
PHP 7.4 or later (8.x recommended), with the pdo extension and the driver for whichever
database you pick below (pdo_mysql, pdo_sqlsrv, or pdo_sqlite)
One of: MySQL/MariaDB, Microsoft SQL Server, or SQLite (no server needed)
A Browser ( ie Firefox or Chrome)


Instructions
---------------------
The easiest way to install UNS is to point your browser at Server/www/install.php.
It will:
  - check that PHP and the required extensions are available
  - let you choose your database (MySQL, SQL Server, or SQLite)
  - create the database/user (or, for SQLite, just the database file) and import
    the matching schema (setup_mysql.sql / setup_sqlsrv.sql / setup_sqlite.sql)
  - create the built-in "unsadmin" account with the password you choose
    (there is no fixed default password - you set one during install)
  - write configs/vars.php and configs/conn.php for you

Once that's done, log in to admin/index.php as the admin account the installer
created for you (a randomised name like unsadmin_a1b2c3, recorded as $admin_user
in configs/vars.php), add more users if you
want LDAP-authenticated logins, and disable the built-in admin account if you'd
rather not keep it around.

If you'd rather set it up by hand instead of using install.php:

    Step 1
Run the setup file matching your database (setup_mysql.sql, setup_sqlsrv.sql,
or setup_sqlite.sql) against it to create the UNS tables.

    Step 2
Create a database user for UNS to connect with (not needed for SQLite).
Permissions needed are all (minus grant) for the UNS database and none for global.

    Step 3
Copy configs/conn.sample.php to configs/conn.php, then edit it - set $driver
to mysql, sqlsrv, or sqlite, and fill in the connection details (or, for
sqlite, just the file path in $db). Only the .sample.php files ship with UNS;
your real configs are never touched by an update.

    Step 4
Copy configs/vars.sample.php to configs/vars.php and edit it.
*See file for details*

    Step 5
Manually insert an admin row into internal_users (password column must be a
password_hash() value) and a matching row into allowed_users with username
'unsadmin', then log in and add more users / disable the built-in admin.

    Step 6
Add Viewing Clients and set them up as you like.

Single sign-on (ADFS, Entra ID, Shibboleth, Okta)
-------------------------------------------------
UNS does not implement SAML or OpenID Connect itself. Instead it trusts an
identity your web server has already established, so every protocol detail,
certificate and signature check stays in a module built for the job. That works
with anything your web server can speak - ADFS or Entra ID over OIDC, a
Shibboleth SP for SAML federations, or IIS Windows authentication.

Only one file needs protecting: admin/sso.php. Leave the rest of the admin
folder unprotected so the built-in administrator can still sign in with a
password. If the identity provider is unreachable, that local login is the only
way back into your own signage - which is exactly when you will want it.

    Step 1
Configure your web server to authenticate admin/sso.php.

Apache with mod_auth_openidc (ADFS 2016+, Entra ID, Keycloak, Okta):

    OIDCProviderMetadataURL https://login.example.edu/adfs/.well-known/openid-configuration
    OIDCClientID            uns
    OIDCClientSecret        <secret>
    OIDCRedirectURI         https://uns.example.edu/uns/admin/redirect_uri
    # ^ must NOT be a real file - mod_auth_openidc serves this path itself, and the
    #   same URL has to be registered with the identity provider
    OIDCCryptoPassphrase    <random string>
    OIDCRemoteUserClaim     preferred_username

    <Files "sso.php">
        AuthType openid-connect
        Require valid-user
    </Files>

Apache with a Shibboleth SP (InCommon and other SAML federations):

    <Files "sso.php">
        AuthType shibboleth
        ShibRequestSetting requireSession 1
        Require valid-user
    </Files>

IIS: enable Windows Authentication on sso.php only, and leave Anonymous
Authentication enabled for the rest of the folder.

    Step 2
In UNS Options, set "Sign-in method" to "Single sign-on via the web server".

    Step 3
Check "Identity server variable". REMOTE_USER is correct for all three setups
above and is what UNS uses by default. The options page shows what the web
server is currently providing, which is the quickest way to confirm the module
is working.

Do not point this at an HTTP_* variable unless a proxy in front of UNS always
overwrites it. Those come from request headers, which means the client sets
them, and anyone could then claim to be anyone. UNS refuses them unless you
explicitly allow header variables.

    Step 4
Decide how accounts are handled. allowed_users stays the authorisation list -
the identity provider only settles who someone is, never what they may do.

  - Leave "Create accounts on first sign-in" off to require that each user is
    added under User Permissions first.
  - Turn it on to have UNS create an account the first time someone signs in.
    New accounts get the ordinary permissions, never Edit Users or UNS Options.

Optionally set a group variable (eg. OIDC_CLAIM_groups) and an administrator
group. Members of that group get Edit Users and UNS Options, and the check runs
at every sign-in, so removing someone at the provider takes effect in UNS too.

    Step 5
Set a logout URL if your provider supports RP-initiated logout. Without one,
logging out of UNS ends only the UNS session - the provider will sign the same
person straight back in.

Active Directory (LDAP) over an encrypted connection
----------------------------------------------------
If you stay on LDAP rather than moving to single sign-on, set "LDAP Encryption"
in UNS Options. UNS collects the user's domain password and binds with it, so
without encryption that password crosses the network in the clear.

  STARTTLS   upgrades a normal connection on port 389. This is usually what you
             want. It is not offered on the Global Catalog port 3268.
  LDAPS      implicit TLS, normally port 636, or 3269 for the Global Catalog.
  None       no encryption. This is the default only so that upgrading UNS does
             not break logins on a domain controller that has no certificate.

Leave "Verify the LDAP certificate" on. Domain controllers usually present a
certificate from an internal CA, which the web server has to trust - set
TLS_CACERT in ldap.conf, or the LDAPTLS_CACERT environment variable, rather than
turning verification off.

UNS will not silently fall back to an unencrypted bind if STARTTLS fails, since
that would hand over the password in the clear at exactly the moment something
is wrong. It reports the failure instead.

